  "generated_text": "1. The perimeter of region I is 12 units. Since region I is a square, the side length of region I is \\(12/4 = 3\\) units.\n2. The perimeter of region II is 24 units. Since region II is a square, the side length of region II is \\(24/4 = 6\\) units.\n3. The area of region I is \\(3^2 = 9\\) square units.\n4. The area of region II is \\(6^2 = 36\\) square units.\n5. The area of region III is the area of region II minus the area of region I, which is \\(36 - 9 = 27\\) square units.\n6. The ratio of the area of region I to the area of region III is \\(\\frac{9}{27}\\).\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the area of region I to the area of region III is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{9}{27}}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[1/3, '\\\\frac{9}{27}']",
    "gold_answer": "A side of square I has length 3, while a side of square II has length 6 (all sides have equal length).  Therefore, a side of square III has length 9.  Since the side length of square I is $\\frac{1}{3}$ that of square III, and the ratio of their areas is the square of the ratio of their side lengths, the ratio of the area of square I to square III is $\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{9}$.   Alternately, you can just calculate the areas: square I has an area of 9, square III has an area of 81, thus, the ratio of their areas is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{9}}$",
    "is_correct": false
  },
